    "narrative": "The Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup is headlined by **James Wood**, whose 76.28 score is the clear standard for today\u2019s slate. Facing a favorable matchup against Lucas Giolito, **James Wood** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill advantage and a prime leadoff spot that maximizes his exposure in a hitter-friendly environment. Following him, **Austin Riley** (64.99) and **Josh Naylor** (63.93) provide high-floor stability; **Austin Riley** is buoyed by a significant park factor and a 13% SP skill bump, while **Josh Naylor** thrives despite facing a respectable arm in Zac Gallen, thanks to a strong platoon edge and cleanup positioning. **Corbin Carroll** remains a critical start (62.47) specifically for his stolen base potential; even against a tough George Kirby, the combination of his speed and the \"Slow SP Delivery\" variable (87% SB success rate) keeps his efficiency score well above the threshold.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the optimizer involved **Cole Young**, who landed just outside the active roster with a 42.80 efficiency score. While his matchup against Zac Gallen offered potential, his projected #7 spot in the lineup proved to be the deciding factor, suppressing his output enough to keep him behind the more efficient platoon advantages offered by the likes of **Zach McKinstry** and **Tyler Soderstrom**. Meanwhile, caution is warranted regarding our catchers, **Dalton Rushing** and **Gabriel Moreno**; both are battling \"Lineup Pending\" designations and face elite arms in Zack Wheeler and George Kirby, respectively. With **Dalton Rushing** facing an xERA of 2.86 and **Gabriel Moreno** dealing with Kirby\u2019s 3.46 mark, we are effectively playing them for their floor, though **Dalton Rushing** does receive a minor boost from projected wind conditions. We are monitoring the status of **Mickey Moniak**, who remains on the IL, while keeping an eye on the volatility surrounding our lineup-pending utility spots to ensure we avoid unnecessary game-cap depletion.",
